.TH XTC_BATCH 3 "2026" "libxtc" "Library Functions"
.SH NAME
xtc_batch_create, xtc_batch_submit, xtc_batch_cancel, xtc_batch_resume,
xtc_batch_close, xtc_batch_destroy, xtc_batch_get_stats \- bounded continuous batching
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <xtc_batch.h>

int xtc_batch_create(xtc_loop_t *loop, const xtc_batch_opts_t *opts,
                    xtc_batch_t **out);
int xtc_batch_submit(xtc_batch_t *group, void *request, uint64_t charge,
                    uint64_t *out_handle);
int xtc_batch_cancel(xtc_batch_t *group, uint64_t handle);
int xtc_batch_resume(xtc_batch_t *group, uint64_t handle);
int xtc_batch_close(xtc_batch_t *group, enum xtc_batch_close_mode mode);
int xtc_batch_destroy(xtc_batch_t *group);
int xtc_batch_get_stats(xtc_batch_t *group, xtc_batch_stats_t *out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A group owns a pinned driver process on the supplied loop. Each synchronous
step callback receives one immutable vector of ready members, not one call per
request. Finished members leave; waiting members refill at subsequent iteration
boundaries. No scheduling policy is required. The API is available when built
with --enable-sched-policy; disabled builds return XTC_E_NOSYS.
.PP
Initialize the size and version fields of options and statistics descriptors
to sizeof the descriptor and XTC_BATCH_VERSION. The descriptor is copied;
callback context and submitted request pointers remain borrowed.
Options specify capacity, max_active, resource_capacity, sched_class,
step, complete, and user. Both callbacks are mandatory.
Capacity must be at least max_active, and both must be positive.
Resource capacity must be positive. A NULL class selects default scheduling;
a supplied class must belong to this loop and cannot be registered to another
batch group. Ordinary non-batch processes may share that class. The class
registration remains reserved until successful group destruction.
.PP
Create is allowed only before the loop's first run or simulation step.
Setup must be externally serialized against execution. All storage is allocated
before publishing the driver; create failure publishes no group. Allocation
size arithmetic is checked, including on 32-bit targets.
.PP
Submit accepts a fixed maximum resource charge in 1..resource_capacity.
Slots include both waiting and active requests. Admission to the active set is
strict first-come, first-served: a younger request cannot bypass an older one
whose charge does not fit. READY and BLOCKED requests both occupy active slots
and retain their resource reservation until their outcome becomes terminal.
Submission itself reserves a slot, not active resource units.
Full or closed groups return XTC_E_AGAIN. Failed submission causes no callback
and leaves the output handle unchanged. Request may be NULL if the application
callback supports it.
.PP
Handles are nonzero, monotonically issued uint64_t identities, never reused
within a group. Exhaustion returns XTC_E_RANGE rather than wrapping. Handles
must be paired with the group that issued them; they are not transferable.
.SH CALLBACKS
The step callback has signature:
.nf
int step(const xtc_batch_item_t *items, xtc_batch_result_t *results,
         size_t count, void *user);
.fi
Each item contains handle and request. Results are initialized to UNSET before
each invocation. Set each state to XTC_BATCH_CONTINUE, XTC_BATCH_DONE,
XTC_BATCH_BLOCKED, or XTC_BATCH_ERROR. ERROR requires an existing nonzero
XTC_E_* error in its error field; other states ignore error.
Omitted or invalid results fail that member with XTC_E_INTERNAL.
A nonzero step return fails the entire current vector with that existing
XTC_E_* error, or XTC_E_INTERNAL for an invalid error value. Neither vectors
nor callback side effects are retried automatically.
.PP
The terminal callback has signature:
.nf
void complete(uint64_t handle, void *request, int outcome, void *user);
.fi
Outcome is XTC_OK for DONE, XTC_E_ABORTED for cancellation, or the recorded
failure. The request borrow lasts through callback return. Completion invocation
is recorded before entering user code; unexpected driver exit never starts the
same notification twice. Recorded terminal outcomes, including DONE, survive
unexpected driver exit. Other outstanding work fails with XTC_E_INTERNAL
(or XTC_E_ABORTED if already cancelled). Such exit closes the group.
.PP
Callbacks run outside runtime bookkeeping and may yield, but must return in
bounded time for progress. While any callback is in flight, ALL mutators on
that group return XTC_E_DEADLK, including peer processes scheduled while the
callback is suspended. Destroy is a mutator. Read-only statistics are allowed
and report committed state, not partially written result words.
Cancellation is masked across callbacks through result recording and completion
accounting. A nonreturning or crashing callback cannot be guaranteed to finish
its own application cleanup; exactly-once invocation does not imply successful
application processing. Arbitrary nonlocal jumps out of the driver are unsupported.
.SH CANCELLATION AND RESUME
Cancel records a request for terminal cancellation at a boundary. A cancellation
recorded before the next vector excludes the request. Already recorded DONE
wins over later cancellation. Stale handles return XTC_E_INVAL after terminal
notification. Duplicate cancellation before notification is harmless.
.PP
Resume changes a live BLOCKED request to READY. Duplicate resume on READY is
harmless. WAITING and terminal requests are not resumable and return XTC_E_INVAL.
Resume does not release or reacquire a blocked request's fixed resource charge.
.SH OWNERSHIP AND SHUTDOWN
Mutation is permitted during externally serialized pre-run setup and, once
started, only from the actual owner loop. Send commands to an owner process
using existing messaging APIs for cross-loop submission. A physical thread
identity is not a loop identity, especially under deterministic simulation.
.PP
XTC_BATCH_DRAIN stops admission and waits for all accepted requests to finish;
BLOCKED requests require explicit resume or cancellation. XTC_BATCH_CANCEL
also cancels all nonterminal requests. Close is idempotent and may escalate
DRAIN to CANCEL, never downgrade it. Close always signals boundary work,
including when the driver is empty or all members are BLOCKED.
.PP
The required lifetime order is close, run until the driver ends, destroy,
then loop or executor finalization. Even closing an empty group before its
first run requires execution of its driver. Stopping a loop or executor is
not draining: destroy returns XTC_E_AGAIN while the driver remains present.
After a stopped run, route close through an owner process and run again.
.PP
Destroy requires a closed group, no borrowed requests or callback, and driver
absence from the process table. An exit hook flag or alive==0 is insufficient:
other exit hooks may still be parked. Destroy and statistics additionally allow
externally synchronized quiescent calls after executor return, with the loop
still allocated. These are not general thread-safe operations; a NULL current
loop does not prove quiescence. Calling loop/exec fini with a live group violates
the lifetime contract and does not promise automatic terminal delivery.
.PP
Normal shutdown uses batch_close, not an asynchronous driver kill. An idle
notify waiter need not wake on kill alone. A later normal batch signal permits
masked waiter unlink before pending kill delivery. There is no hidden polling
thread, periodic wake, real clock read, or unseeded randomness in the engine.
.SH STATISTICS
The snapshot includes queued, ready, blocked, reserved_units, iterations,
completed, cancelled, failed, rejections, max_vector, and total_members.
Queued means waiting for admission; ready and blocked count active members.
Iteration and membership totals measure vector calls and their occupancy.
Terminal counters advance after completion delivery returns (or after an
interrupted delivery is retired). Rejections count invalid or unsuccessful
submissions after owner/callback validation. Cumulative counters saturate at
UINT64_MAX. Per-request timing belongs in application instrumentation.
.SH ERRORS
XTC_E_INVAL indicates malformed arguments, descriptor size, foreign ownership,
foreign class, invalid close mode, or an unavailable handle.
XTC_E_VERSION indicates an unsupported descriptor version.
XTC_E_RANGE indicates charge, allocation arithmetic, or handle exhaustion.
XTC_E_AGAIN indicates full/closed admission, creation after first execution,
or destruction before shutdown has completed.
XTC_E_DEADLK indicates mutation during a callback.
XTC_E_NOMEM indicates allocation failure during create.
.SH SEE ALSO
